## 2026-08-19 — Task: OBBBA senior deduction (tax years 2025–2028)

Modeled the temporary $6,000-per-senior deduction and its 6% MAGI phaseout, so
the app now shows the second hump stacked on the SS torpedo.

### What the statute actually says (verified, not assumed)

IRC 151(d)(5), added by P.L. 119-21 sec. 70103:

  "In the case of a taxable year beginning before January 1, 2029, there shall
   be allowed a deduction in an amount equal to $6,000 for each qualified
   individual with respect to the taxpayer."
  "The $6,000 amount in clause (i) shall be reduced (but not below zero) by
   6 percent of so much of the taxpayer's modified adjusted gross income as
   exceeds $75,000 ($150,000 in the case of a joint return)."

THE KEY READING: the reduction applies to *each qualified individual's own*
$6,000, not to the couple's combined $12,000. That is what reconciles the
statute with the widely published "fully gone at $175,000 / $250,000":

  single, 1 senior:  6,000 / 0.06  = 100,000 -> 75,000 + 100,000 = 175,000
  mfj,    1 senior:  6,000 / 0.06  = 100,000 -> 150,000 + 100,000 = 250,000
  mfj,    2 seniors: 12,000 / 0.12 = 100,000 -> 150,000 + 100,000 = 250,000

If you instead reduced the combined $12,000 by 6%, MFJ would run to $350,000
and contradict every published source. The phaseout range is $100,000 wide for
every filing status and every senior count. Sources: IRS newsroom "Check your
eligibility for the new enhanced deduction for seniors" (amount, years,
thresholds, itemizer-agnostic), law.cornell.edu 26 USC 151 (statutory text),
Thomson Reuters / Tax Foundation (the $175k/$250k endpoints).

MAGI here = AGI + the sec. 911/931/933 foreign exclusions, which this app does
not model — so MAGI is just AGI. Tax-exempt interest is NOT added back. That is
a *different* MAGI from IRMAA's (AGI + tax-exempt interest); the muni-interest
task must keep the two apart, and there is a comment in seniorDeductionFor
saying so.

No circularity: the senior deduction phases out on AGI, and it is taken *from*
AGI rather than added to it. AGI is fully determined (including taxable SS)
before any deduction is applied.

### API

New in tax.ts: SENIOR_DEDUCTION (6_000), SENIOR_DEDUCTION_FIRST_YEAR / _LAST_YEAR
(2025 / 2028), SENIOR_DEDUCTION_PHASEOUT_RATE (0.06),
SENIOR_DEDUCTION_PHASEOUT_START ({single: 75_000, mfj: 150_000}),
seniorDeductionPhaseoutEnd(fs), seniorDeductionFor(fs, seniors, magi), and
deductionFor(fs, seniors, magi) = standardDeductionFor + seniorDeductionFor.

NO new positional parameter was added anywhere — the deduction is derived from
the `seniors` count that last iteration already threaded through everything,
plus a MAGI that each call site already had in hand. Call sites changed:
totalTax, totalTaxWithLTCG (renamed its local `standardDeduction` -> `deduction`)
and conversionMeasureValue. standardDeductionFor() is unchanged and still means
*only* the standard deduction, which is what the UI's "Standard deduction $X"
readout shows; deductionFor() is the full stack.

Also factored the senior-count clamp out of standardDeductionFor into a private
seniorCount() helper so both deductions clamp identically.

Binary search in maxConversionUnder is still valid: a bigger conversion only
ever shrinks the deduction, so taxable income remains non-decreasing (comment
added to conversionMeasureValue).

### The numbers this pinned down

Marginal-rate arithmetic, which is the whole point of the feature:

- Inside the phaseout, taxable income rises $1.06 per dollar earned (single, or
  MFJ with one senior) or $1.12 (MFJ, both seniors). 22% bites at 23.32% /
  24.64%; 24% bites at 25.44%. A surtax that is on no rate schedule.
- IT MULTIPLIES WITH THE TORPEDO. MAGI includes the SS the torpedo drags in, so
  in the overlap a dollar earned is 1.85 x 1.06 = $1.96 of taxable income and
  22% becomes 43.14% (vs the torpedo's own 40.7%). MFJ both-seniors: 1.85 x 1.12
  = $2.07 and 45.58%. Verified against totalTax at single/max benefit/$50,000.
- Overlap only happens for a single filer at large benefits: MFJ needs MAGI
  > $150,000 while SS is still uncapped, and with the app's single-earner max
  benefit the 85% cap binds at ~$67,589 of other income (MAGI ~$118,600), so
  MFJ never overlaps. Do not write an MFJ overlap test expecting 45.58% from
  totalTax; the 1.12 factor is testable on its own at MFJ/$150,000.
- Right edge of the hump: for single + average benefit, phaseout ends at
  $175,000 MAGI = ~$154,845 of other income, i.e. just off the 150k chart. At
  the maximum benefit it lands at ~$122,900 of income and IS on-chart. So the
  explainer computes `phaseoutEndsOnChart` from
  MAX_INCOME + taxableSocialSecurity(...) instead of asserting either way.
  I deliberately did NOT widen MAX_INCOME to make the hump's right edge always
  visible: it would compress the actual torpedo (0–60k) for everyone, and a
  seniors-dependent axis is worse. Revisit if the multi-year task needs it.

Concrete regressions (all now tests):
- First taxed dollar, single, no benefits: 15,751 (no senior) -> 23,751.
- Deduction saving, single/$30k/avg benefit: (2,000 + 6,000) x 12% = $960.
- MFJ had to move from $30,000 to $60,000 for the saving test: at $30,000 the
  couple's taxable income (3,428) runs out before the deduction does, so the
  old "1,600 x 10%" assertion no longer isolates anything. At $60,000 it is
  (1,600 + 6,000) x 12% = $912 per qualifying spouse.
- 0%-rate valley, single/avg benefit: last zero-rate sample 14,750 -> 20,000,
  exact crossing 14,881 -> 20,214. Still the 1.5x rule: 8,000 / 1.5 = 5,333.
- Single, $100k of gains only: 5,385 (no senior) -> 4,410. $100k of AGI is
  $25k into the phaseout, so only $4,500 of the $6,000 survives, and
  (2,000 + 4,500) x 15% = $975 = 5,385 - 4,410. This test now exercises the
  phaseout by accident of the gain size — noted in its comment.
- Conversion to the top of the 12% bracket, single/$30k/avg: 16,069 -> 22,069
  (+8,000 exactly, because MAGI at the ceiling is $72,224, still short of
  $75,000). taxBefore 2,573 -> 1,853, taxAfter unchanged at 5,578, cost per
  dollar 18.70c -> 16.88c.
- Conversion to the top of the 22% bracket is the one that shows the phaseout
